<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  JawaTerrain_SaltPans.xml               Jawa_Patches · authored 2026-08-13
  ============================================================================

  V1 ROW 4, override 1 of 3 (the attachment half).

  Defs/TerrainDefs/JawaSaltCrust.xml declares Jawa_SaltCrust. A TerrainDef on
  its own generates NOWHERE, exactly as a WeatherDef on its own blows nowhere
  (see SWDesertWeather_Attach.xml, same mod, same lesson). A biome must ask
  for it. This file is that ask.

  WHAT THE PLAYER SEES: broad, pale, cracked salt pans lying in the low ground
  of desert maps, unmistakably not sand, flat enough to build and land on.

  ===========================================================================
  THE MECHANISM, READ FROM CORE, NOT INFERRED
  ===========================================================================
  Core/Defs/BiomeDefs/Biomes_WarmArid.xml, biome Desert, lines 256-269:

      <terrainPatchMakers>
        <li>
          <perlinFrequency>0.05</perlinFrequency>
          <maxFertility>0.8</maxFertility>
          <minSize>13</minSize>
          <thresholds>
            <li>
              <terrain>SoftSand</terrain>
              <min>0.65</min>
              <max>999</max>
            </li>
          </thresholds>
        </li>
      </terrainPatchMakers>

  terrainPatchMakers is a PLAIN LIST of TerrainPatchMaker, so <li> is correct
  here. That is not a guess: the node above already contains <li>, and the
  child shape is decided by the field's C# type. Contrast the sibling field
  <baseWeatherCommonalities>, which is dictionary keyed and where an <li>
  destroyed seven biomes (SWDesertWeather_Attach.xml documents the wreckage).
  <thresholds> is likewise a plain list. Both confirmed by reading the node.

  Tuning, and why these numbers:
    perlinFrequency 0.03  LOWER than Core's 0.05 = larger, fewer features.
                          A salt pan is one big lake bed, not a speckle.
    maxFertility 0.5      pans only form on the driest ground, so they never
                          eat the rare fertile corners of AridShrubland.
    minSize 25            Core uses 13. A pan smaller than this is noise.
    min 0.70              the top of the perlin field only. Combined with
                          maxFertility this lands around a tenth of a desert
                          map, which is "obvious at a glance" without being
                          the whole map. THIS is the number to move if the
                          owner wants more or less salt; nothing else here
                          is load bearing.

  ===========================================================================
  SAFETY
  ===========================================================================
  Desert and ExtremeDesert already own a <terrainPatchMakers> node, so those
  are plain Conditional + Add with an identical test and inner xpath.

  AridShrubland has NO terrainPatchMakers node at all (verified: its def runs
  Biomes_WarmArid.xml:4-169 and contains terrainsByFertility at :80 but no
  patch makers). Adding a CHILD to a node that does not exist is the exact
  failure logged in traps-xml-and-defs.md, "One failed op silently kills every
  op after it in the same sequence". So AridShrubland is guarded on the
  CONTAINER: <match> adds the li, <nomatch> adds the whole element. This is
  the only place in this file where the test xpath and the inner xpath
  deliberately differ, and that is what a Conditional is for.

  The sibling file JawaTerrain_DuneSeas.xml guards AridShrubland the same way.
  Whichever of the two applies first creates the node; the other then takes
  the <match> branch. Order independent by construction.

  ⚠️ GEOLOGICAL LANDFORMS WINS ON LANDFORM TILES. READ BEFORE "FIXING" THIS.
  =========================================================================
  Geological Landforms (m00nl1ght.geologicallandforms, load 156) Harmony
  patches RimWorld.TerrainPatchMaker itself. Symbols in
  ...294100/2773943594/1.6/Lunar/Components/GeologicalLandforms.dll include
  Patch_RimWorld_TerrainPatchMaker, NodeTerrainFromBiome, TpmTerrainAt.

  What that means in practice, and it is NOT a reason to change this file:
    - On an ORDINARY tile, GL's NodeTerrainFromBiome READS the biome's
      terrainPatchMakers, so the salt pans below generate normally.
    - On a tile carrying a LANDFORM, that landform's node graph can hard
      write terrain over the biome result. 33 of GL's ~50 landform files
      contain an outputTerrain node.

  The specific irony, worth knowing before anyone hunts it: GL's own dry lake
  landform hard codes SoftSand and Sand at
  ...294100/2773943594/1.6/Landforms-v1/LandformDryLake.xml:290-291
  So the ONE map feature that ought to be a salt pan is generated as soft
  sand by GL, and this patch cannot reach it. Fixing that means editing a
  serialised NodeCanvas (target Objects/Object[@refID="0"]/ArrayOfString/
  string[1]), which is not thin v1 work. Filed, not done.

  Scope note: patched to the three CORE arid biomes only. Modded desert
  biomes (ZBiome_Badlands, ZBiome_DesertOasis, the Alpha Biomes arid set) are
  deliberately NOT patched here. That is a v1 thinness call, not an oversight;
  it is one extra Operation each if the owner wants wider coverage later.
  ============================================================================
-->
<Patch>

  <!-- Core: Desert -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="Desert"]/terrainPatchMakers</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="Desert"]/terrainPatchMakers</xpath>
      <value>
        <li>
          <perlinFrequency>0.03</perlinFrequency>
          <maxFertility>0.5</maxFertility>
          <minSize>25</minSize>
          <thresholds>
            <li>
              <terrain>Jawa_SaltCrust</terrain>
              <min>0.70</min>
              <max>999</max>
            </li>
          </thresholds>
        </li>
      </value>
    </match>
  </Operation>

  <!-- Core: Extreme Desert -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="ExtremeDesert"]/terrainPatchMakers</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="ExtremeDesert"]/terrainPatchMakers</xpath>
      <value>
        <li>
          <perlinFrequency>0.03</perlinFrequency>
          <maxFertility>0.5</maxFertility>
          <minSize>25</minSize>
          <thresholds>
            <li>
              <terrain>Jawa_SaltCrust</terrain>
              <min>0.68</min>
              <max>999</max>
            </li>
          </thresholds>
        </li>
      </value>
    </match>
  </Operation>

  <!--
    Core: Arid Shrubland. No terrainPatchMakers node exists on this def, so
    the <nomatch> branch has to create the whole element. See SAFETY above.
  -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="AridShrubland"]/terrainPatchMakers</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="AridShrubland"]/terrainPatchMakers</xpath>
      <value>
        <li>
          <perlinFrequency>0.03</perlinFrequency>
          <maxFertility>0.4</maxFertility>
          <minSize>25</minSize>
          <thresholds>
            <li>
              <terrain>Jawa_SaltCrust</terrain>
              <min>0.75</min>
              <max>999</max>
            </li>
          </thresholds>
        </li>
      </value>
    </match>
    <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="AridShrubland"]</xpath>
      <value>
        <terrainPatchMakers>
          <li>
            <perlinFrequency>0.03</perlinFrequency>
            <maxFertility>0.4</maxFertility>
            <minSize>25</minSize>
            <thresholds>
              <li>
                <terrain>Jawa_SaltCrust</terrain>
                <min>0.75</min>
                <max>999</max>
              </li>
            </thresholds>
          </li>
        </terrainPatchMakers>
      </value>
    </nomatch>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
